{
  "recap": "In today's class, we learned about two powerful tactical moves: the pin and the skewer. A pin happens when a piece is stuck because a more valuable piece is behind it, while a skewer is the opposite, where a valuable piece is attacked first and must move, leaving a piece behind it to be captured. We discussed how to use these tactics to trap our opponents and how to defend our own pieces from being caught in these lines. Remember that only pieces that move in straight lines can perform these moves.",
  "keyPoints": [
    "A pin freezes a piece in place because moving it would expose a more valuable piece behind it.",
    "An absolute pin involves the king, making it illegal to move the pinned piece.",
    "A skewer attacks a valuable piece first, forcing it to move so you can capture the piece behind it.",
    "Always check if your king and queen are on the same line to avoid falling for a skewer."
  ],
  "practice": [
    "Complete the assigned pin and skewer puzzles before Saturday.",
    "Before every move in your games, ask yourself if any of your pieces are currently being pinned."
  ]
}
